  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Archetypometrics, a Pragmateia: Empirical Determination of the Fundamental Archetypes of Fictional Characters (2025) in Zenodo. 3272 downloads.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Archetypometrics, a Pragmateia: Empirical Determination of the Fundamental Archetypes of Fictional Characters (2025) in Zenodo is authored by Dodds, Peter Sheridan, Zimmerman, Julia Witte and Beauregard, Calla G.. Topics: Archetype, Storytelling, Computer science, Quality (philosophy) and Personality.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 article is already showing active use, with 3,272 recorded downloads and 1,075 views, and it has one documented downstream reuse in the scholarly record. The citation record is still early, so the clearest evidence of influence at this stage comes from access and reuse rather than formal citation accumulation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Archetypometrics, a Pragmateia presents an empirical framework for identifying base archetypes in fictional characters across film, television, and literature. The paper\u2019s central claim is methodological as well as substantive: it argues that character-centric storytelling can be studied systematically, and it reports six primary and six secondary archetypes that are extended into a broader framework of single, dual, and triple archetypes.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal so far is sustained readership. Recorded downloads substantially exceed views, suggesting that readers are not only encountering the work but retrieving it for closer inspection. That pattern fits the paper\u2019s unusually explicit invitation to be read in a desktop or laptop PDF viewer and its broad conceptual reach across storytelling, narrative analysis, personality, and related computational approaches.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also one documented downstream reuse in the record, indicating that the work has already entered another scholarly context. The reuse trail includes a biomedical article mention alongside linked Zenodo versions and related research objects, which suggests the project is being treated as a reusable research resource rather than as a standalone essay only.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record remains limited, with no OpenAlex citations yet and no scite citation confirmations in the available data. That makes this a work with visible uptake but still early formal scholarly reception. Public-discourse signals are not available from the archive, so the record currently supports a story of access, reuse, and emerging scholarly attention more than one of established citation impact.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}